//! check: fail(E1010)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: mem.region.escape, mem.str.view
//!
//! s171 (wolf-lang#392) — a `[mem.str.view]` product carries its
//! receiver's sites, so a view of a region-built `str` cannot outlive
//! the region whose bytes it names.
//!
//! The clause said "a slice and every `[mem.str.view]` product
//! allocate nothing" and that every yielded `str` "is a subslice of
//! the receiver's own storage", and left unstated whether a view
//! carries the receiver's SITES. Read as "allocates nothing, so no
//! site", this file returned a view into bytes `scratch` had already
//! freed — `exit(0)`, printing `regions`, on all three lanes, and it
//! printed correctly only because the arena had not been reused.
//!
//! What decides it is the asymmetry: `s` itself leaving `scratch` was
//! already E1010 (`region_str_concat_return.lu`), while `s.trim()` —
//! the same bytes, one word narrower — left clean. Allocating nothing
//! and pointing nowhere are different properties: "allocates nothing"
//! governs the ACCOUNT, and a view still charges zero, so `#[noalloc]`
//! is untouched.
//!
//! The legal companions are `region_str_view_inside.lu` (the view used
//! within the region that owns it) and a view of a LITERAL, whose
//! bytes are static and which is no site at all.

fn build() -> str {
    region scratch {
        let s = "  re" + "gions  "
        let t = s.trim()
        t
    }
}

fn main() -> !int {
    print("{build()}")
    0
}
